# High-Performance Stochastic Turbulence Calibration Engine

## Project Mission

Build a production-oriented, high-performance C++20/CUDA platform for simulating stochastically forced turbulence, calibrating unknown model parameters from sparse noisy observations, reconstructing hidden flow states, quantifying uncertainty through Bayesian inference, and generating probabilistic predictions.

The project must combine:

* Computational fluid dynamics
* Stochastic processes
* Numerical optimization
* Statistical calibration
* Bayesian inference
* Monte Carlo simulation
* Sensitivity analysis
* State estimation
* Reduced-order modeling
* Surrogate modeling
* Rare-event estimation
* Modern C++ software engineering
* CPU, GPU, and distributed high-performance computing
* Numerical, statistical, and performance validation

The primary physical application is two-dimensional incompressible turbulence in a periodic domain. However, an important purpose of the project is to develop the mathematical, statistical, numerical, and C++ engineering skills required for a Quantitative C++ Engineer position in finance.

The project should deliberately incorporate methods commonly used in quantitative finance whenever they have a valid mathematical or scientific role in stochastic turbulence. It should not avoid a technique merely because it is associated with finance.

The governing principle is:

> Use as many methods relevant to financial quantitative C++ engineering as possible, provided each method has a legitimate mathematical, statistical, computational, or software-engineering role in the turbulence project.

The finished system should be a genuine fluid-dynamics project and a comprehensive quantitative-computing platform.

---

# Why This Project Was Selected

Among fluid-dynamics projects, stochastic turbulence calibration most closely resembles the computational workflow of an advanced financial quantitative-modeling system.

A conventional CFD program answers:

> Given physical parameters and initial conditions, how does the fluid evolve?

This project asks a more difficult quantitative question:

> Given incomplete and noisy observations of a turbulent system, what parameters and hidden states most likely generated the observations, how uncertain are those estimates, and what probability distribution describes future behavior?

The central workflow is:

$$
\boxed{
\text{observations}
\rightarrow
\text{mathematical model}
\rightarrow
\text{calibration}
\rightarrow
\text{sensitivity}
\rightarrow
\text{Bayesian inference}
\rightarrow
\text{uncertainty propagation}
\rightarrow
\text{probabilistic prediction}
}
$$

This resembles quantitative financial model development because both domains require:

* Translating mathematical equations into reliable C++
* Calibrating models against noisy observations
* Working with hidden stochastic states
* Estimating uncertain parameters
* Calculating sensitivities
* Running large scenario ensembles
* Constructing reduced or surrogate models
* Estimating tail probabilities
* Optimizing repeated model evaluations
* Validating numerical and statistical results

The conceptual correspondence is:

| Quantitative finance concept         | Turbulence project equivalent                 |
| ------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------- |
| Mathematical pricing or market model | Stochastic Navier–Stokes model                |
| Hidden market factors                | Unobserved turbulent modes                    |
| Volatility or stochastic intensity   | Turbulence and forcing intensity              |
| Mean-reverting factor                | Ornstein–Uhlenbeck forcing mode               |
| Market observations                  | Sparse velocity or vorticity observations     |
| Model calibration                    | Estimation of physical and forcing parameters |
| Scenario generation                  | Turbulence ensemble simulation                |
| Financial sensitivities              | Sensitivities of energy and dissipation       |
| Tail-loss probability                | Extreme-dissipation probability               |
| Pricing surrogate                    | Reduced-order turbulence model                |
| Bayesian parameter posterior         | Posterior distribution of physical parameters |
| Sequential model update              | Flow-state update as observations arrive      |
| Stress testing                       | Extreme forcing and parameter scenarios       |
| Parallel scenario engine             | CPU, CUDA, and MPI ensemble execution         |

---

# Scope Principle

The project does not need to hide its relationship to quantitative finance. Its application is fluid dynamics, but its architecture should intentionally exercise techniques used by financial quantitative developers.

Domain-independent finance methods should be included whenever they fit the turbulence problem, including:

* Stochastic differential equations
* Factor models
* Mean-reverting processes
* Correlated Brownian motion
* Maximum-likelihood estimation
* Bayesian calibration
* Monte Carlo simulation
* Quasi-Monte Carlo
* Multilevel Monte Carlo
* Antithetic variates
* Control variates
* Importance sampling
* Tail-probability estimation
* Sensitivity calculations
* Automatic differentiation
* Adjoint differentiation
* Gradient-based optimization
* Constrained calibration
* State-space models
* Kalman and particle filtering
* Time-series analysis
* Regime detection
* Scenario generation
* Stress testing
* Surrogate models
* Probabilistic forecasting
* High-performance parallel evaluation

Purely market-specific components should be omitted when they do not have a coherent role. For example, an exchange protocol, order book, portfolio-accounting system, or trading execution strategy would not contribute meaningfully to the turbulence problem.

Every included method must solve a real problem involving turbulence simulation, calibration, inference, forecasting, sensitivity, uncertainty, tail behavior, or computational performance.

---

# Physical Problem

Model statistically stationary, stochastically forced, two-dimensional incompressible turbulence in a square periodic domain:

$$
\Omega=[0,2\pi]\times[0,2\pi].
$$

Periodic boundary conditions apply in both directions.

The system continuously receives energy through stochastic forcing. Nonlinear interactions transfer energy between spatial scales. Viscosity removes energy at small scales, while large-scale friction controls energy accumulation at large scales.

A periodic domain is preferred because it provides:

* A fundamental fluid-physics problem
* No application-specific geometry
* No mesh-generation problem
* Clean Fourier representations
* Efficient spectral differentiation
* Efficient FFT-based computation
* Natural statistical homogeneity
* Straightforward CPU and CUDA acceleration

The engine must determine how the statistical behavior of the turbulent system depends on unknown physical and stochastic parameters.

---

# Governing Equations

The incompressible Navier–Stokes equations are:

$$
\frac{\partial\mathbf u}{\partial t}
+
(\mathbf u\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u
=
-\nabla p
+
\nu\nabla^2\mathbf u
-
\alpha\mathbf u
+
\mathbf f(\mathbf x,t),
$$

subject to:

$$
\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=0.
$$

Here:

* \(\mathbf u(\mathbf x,t)\) is velocity.
* \(p(\mathbf x,t)\) is pressure.
* \(\nu\) is kinematic viscosity.
* \(\alpha\) is large-scale linear friction.
* \(\mathbf f(\mathbf x,t)\) is stochastic forcing.

For the two-dimensional periodic implementation, use the vorticity-streamfunction formulation:

$$
\frac{\partial\omega}{\partial t}
+
\mathbf u\cdot\nabla\omega
=
\nu\nabla^2\omega
-
\alpha\omega
+
f_\omega.
$$

The streamfunction satisfies:

$$
\nabla^2\psi=-\omega.
$$

Velocity is reconstructed using:

$$
u=\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial y},
\qquad
v=-\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial x}.
$$

This removes pressure from the primary evolution equation and permits an efficient Fourier-space Poisson solution.

---

# Stochastic Forcing Model

The forcing must have a precise mathematical definition. It should not be arbitrary independent noise added to every grid point.

Use band-limited stochastic forcing acting on a selected Fourier wave-number shell:

$$
k_{\min}\leq |\mathbf k|\leq k_{\max}.
$$

The forcing amplitudes may follow Ornstein–Uhlenbeck processes:

$$
dF_{\mathbf k}
=
-\frac{1}{\tau_f}F_{\mathbf k}\,dt
+
\sigma_f\,dW_{\mathbf k}.
$$

Here:

* \(\sigma_f\) controls forcing intensity.
* \(\tau_f\) controls temporal correlation.
* \(W_{\mathbf k}\) represents Wiener processes.
* The forcing exhibits mean reversion and correlated stochastic evolution.

The forcing implementation must:

* Preserve conjugate symmetry for real physical fields
* Support deterministic seeding
* Support independent parallel random streams
* Reproduce expected stationary variance
* Reproduce expected autocorrelation
* Allow configurable spatial and temporal correlation

Optional extensions may support correlated forcing modes and non-Gaussian shocks when they have a clear physical interpretation.

---

# Unknown Parameters

The initial calibration target should be a continuous parameter vector such as:

$$
\theta=
(\nu,\alpha,\sigma_f,\tau_f).
$$

Possible later parameters include:

* Spatial forcing correlation length
* Observation-noise variance
* Model-discrepancy parameters
* Reduced-order closure coefficients
* Initial reduced-state coefficients
* Cross-correlation parameters between stochastic modes

Parameters constrained to be positive should use bound constraints or transformations:

$$
\nu=e^{q_\nu},
\qquad
\alpha=e^{q_\alpha},
\qquad
\sigma_f=e^{q_\sigma},
\qquad
\tau_f=e^{q_\tau}.
$$

Discrete model choices, such as the forcing wave-number shell, should initially remain configuration values rather than differentiable calibration parameters.

---

# Full-Order Turbulence Solver

The full-order reference model should use a pseudo-spectral discretization.

It should include:

* Fourier representation of vorticity
* Forward and inverse FFTs
* Spectral spatial derivatives
* Fourier-space Poisson solution
* Velocity reconstruction
* Physical-space nonlinear advection
* \(2/3\) dealiasing
* Stable time integration
* CFL stability monitoring
* Configurable grid resolution
* Configurable simulation duration
* Checkpoint and restart
* Physical and spectral diagnostics

Potential time integrators include:

* Explicit Runge–Kutta
* Semi-implicit Runge–Kutta
* Integrating-factor methods
* Exponential time differencing

The selected method must be justified through stability, accuracy, and performance analysis.

---

# Physical and Statistical Observables

The engine should calculate the following quantities.

## Kinetic Energy

$$
E(t)
=
\frac12
\int_\Omega
|\mathbf u(\mathbf x,t)|^2\,d\mathbf x.
$$

## Enstrophy

$$
Z(t)
=
\frac12
\int_\Omega
\omega(\mathbf x,t)^2\,d\mathbf x.
$$

## Energy Dissipation

$$
\varepsilon(t)
=
\nu
\int_\Omega
|\nabla\mathbf u|^2\,d\mathbf x.
$$

## Energy Spectrum

$$
E(k),
$$

computed by grouping Fourier modes into wave-number shells.

## Additional Statistics

Support:

* Mean energy and enstrophy
* Variance of energy and enstrophy
* Dissipation distributions
* Vorticity distributions
* Autocorrelation functions
* Cross-correlation functions
* Integral correlation times
* Power spectral densities
* Structure functions
* Quantiles
* Extreme-event frequencies
* Threshold-crossing times

These observables should serve as calibration targets, validation metrics, forecast outputs, and rare-event quantities.

---

# Synthetic Observation System

The project should support controlled synthetic-data experiments.

A reference simulation should run with a hidden parameter vector:

$$
\theta_{\text{true}}.
$$

Observations are generated through:

$$
y_k
=
H_k(q(t_k;\theta_{\text{true}}))
+
\epsilon_k,
$$

where:

* \(q\) is the full or reduced fluid state.
* \(H_k\) is an observation operator.
* \(\epsilon_k\) is measurement noise.

Observations may include:

* Sparse velocity probes
* Sparse vorticity probes
* Global kinetic energy
* Global enstrophy
* Dissipation
* Energy-spectrum bins
* Autocorrelation statistics
* Irregularly sampled time-series data

The observation system should support:

* Gaussian noise
* Heteroscedastic uncertainty
* Correlated measurement noise
* Missing observations
* Irregular timestamps
* Configurable outliers
* Separate calibration and validation periods

The calibration engine must not have direct access to the hidden parameter values.

---

# Calibration Objective

The objective may combine local observations and global statistical properties:

$$
J(\theta)
=
w_sJ_{\text{sensor}}
+
w_eJ_{\text{energy}}
+
w_zJ_{\text{enstrophy}}
+
w_pJ_{\text{spectrum}}
+
w_cJ_{\text{correlation}}
+
\lambda R(\theta).
$$

A weighted sensor objective is:

$$
J_{\text{sensor}}
=
\frac12
\sum_k
\left[
H_kq(t_k;\theta)-y_k
\right]^T
R_k^{-1}
\left[
H_kq(t_k;\theta)-y_k
\right].
$$

A spectral objective is:

$$
J_{\text{spectrum}}
=
\sum_j
w_j
\left[
E_{\text{model}}(k_j;\theta)
-
E_{\text{observed}}(k_j)
\right]^2.
$$

The calibration system should support:

* Weighted least squares
* Negative log likelihood
* Maximum-likelihood estimation
* Maximum a posteriori estimation
* Regularization
* Parameter constraints
* Parameter transformations
* Multiple initial guesses
* Held-out validation
* Residual analysis
* Identifiability analysis

Optimization convergence must not automatically be interpreted as successful physical parameter recovery.

---

# Numerical Optimization

Support and compare relevant optimization methods:

* Grid search for low-dimensional verification
* Random search
* Brent or golden-section search
* Nelder–Mead
* Gradient descent with line search
* Nonlinear conjugate gradient
* Gauss–Newton
* Levenberg–Marquardt
* BFGS
* L-BFGS
* Trust-region methods
* Bound-constrained optimization
* Multi-start optimization
* Global-search followed by local refinement

At least one meaningful optimization algorithm should be implemented internally. Established libraries may also be used for production calculations and independent validation.

Expose:

* Objective history
* Parameter history
* Gradient norms
* Step sizes
* Function-evaluation counts
* Convergence reasons
* Failure reasons
* Runtime
* Accuracy against hidden parameters

---

# Sensitivity Analysis and Differentiation

The project should calculate how outputs and objectives respond to parameter changes.

Examples include:

$$
\frac{\partial\mathbb E[E]}{\partial\nu},
\qquad
\frac{\partial\mathbb E[Z]}{\partial\alpha},
\qquad
\frac{\partial P(\varepsilon>\varepsilon_c)}
{\partial\sigma_f}.
$$

Support and compare the following approaches.

## Finite Differences

$$
\frac{\partial J}{\partial\theta_i}
\approx
\frac{
J(\theta+h\mathbf e_i)-J(\theta-h\mathbf e_i)
}{
2h
}.
$$

Study truncation and cancellation errors as the step size changes.

## Forward Sensitivities

Differentiate the model equations with respect to individual parameters.

## Automatic Differentiation

Use an appropriate C++ automatic-differentiation library for compatible model components.

## Adjoint Differentiation

For:

$$
F(q,\theta)=0,
$$

with objective \(J(q,\theta)\), solve:

$$
\left(
\frac{\partial F}{\partial q}
\right)^T\lambda
=
-
\left(
\frac{\partial J}{\partial q}
\right)^T.
$$

Then calculate:

$$
\frac{dJ}{d\theta}
=
\frac{\partial J}{\partial\theta}
+
\lambda^T
\frac{\partial F}{\partial\theta}.
$$

The engine should compare:

* Gradient accuracy
* Runtime
* Memory requirements
* Scaling with parameter count

Gradient checking is mandatory before gradients are used in calibration or Hamiltonian Monte Carlo.

