# Reduced-Order Modeling

Build a reduced-order representation to accelerate calibration, inference, filtering, and uncertainty propagation.

Collect snapshots:

$$
X=
\begin{bmatrix}
| & | & & |\\
q_1 & q_2 & \cdots & q_N\\
| & | & & |
\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Apply singular-value decomposition:

$$
X=U\Sigma V^T.
$$

Construct a POD approximation:

$$
q(\mathbf x,t)
\approx
\bar q(\mathbf x)
+
\sum_{i=1}^{r}
a_i(t)\phi_i(\mathbf x).
$$

Support:

* Snapshot collection
* Mean subtraction
* SVD
* Energy-based mode selection
* POD basis construction
* Projection
* Reconstruction
* Galerkin evolution
* Closure modeling
* Stability correction
* Full-order versus reduced-order validation

The reduced model must be validated over parameter regions used for calibration and inference.

---

# Surrogate Modeling

Support fast approximations for expensive model evaluations.

Possible methods include:

* Polynomial response surfaces
* Radial-basis interpolation
* Gaussian-process regression
* Small neural networks
* Neural operators as an optional extension

Surrogates may predict:

* Calibration objectives
* Statistical observables
* Reduced-state evolution
* Parameter-to-spectrum mappings
* Rare-event probabilities

Evaluate:

* Training error
* Held-out error
* Extrapolation failure
* Prediction uncertainty
* Speedup
* Calibration bias
* Posterior bias

For Bayesian inference, optionally implement delayed-acceptance MCMC:

1. Evaluate a proposal with the inexpensive surrogate.
2. Reject clearly poor proposals early.
3. Evaluate promising proposals using the full model.
4. Apply a correction that preserves the intended posterior.

